# Dossier update instructions

This repo exists to publish one unlisted page: `s/f9a3b7c2d6e4f1a0b3c7d9e2/index.html`. Treat that file as the product. Do not invent a build step, split it into components, or restyle it unless asked.

## What this is

A stats tracker and a lessons-learned log for one Rainbow Six Siege squad, kept as a single self-contained HTML page.

Two jobs, and they are separate:

- **Tracker.** Current career figures per player and per operator, refreshed from tracker exports.
- **Lessons learned.** What the figures mean, what has been tried, what worked, what did not, and what is still an open question.

The tracker half is data. The lessons half is judgement. Keep them in their own places — figures in tables, judgement in prose — and never duplicate one inside the other.

The document is descriptive, not aspirational. Every claim should be traceable to rounds actually played. The recurring test for keeping an operator is **fun + performance + repeatability**: enjoyment is a legitimate selection criterion, because enjoyment drives volume and volume drives competence.

## Privacy

- The repo is public so GitHub Pages can host it. The URL is the only access control.
- Never put the published URL, the folder slug, or a hyperlink to the page in any public place (README, commits that leak the path into GitHub search, tweets, Discord, etc. are already a judgement call — do not add new public links).
- Keep `<meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow">` on the page if it is present or requested. Do not Disallow the path in `robots.txt`; crawlers must fetch the page to see noindex.
- **Players are referred to by handle only.** Real names were deliberately redacted from this document. Never reintroduce one, including in comments, commit messages or prose.

## Where to edit

The page is a single HTML file: static markup in the body, then a `<script>` that renders most of the live content.

Edit **data**, not the render templates, unless the layout itself is wrong.

| Change | Edit this |
|---|---|
| Player cards, strengths, weaknesses, suggestions, avoid, operator tables | `const P = [...]` |
| Squad-at-a-glance table | `const ROSTER = [...]` (keep in sync with each player's `strip`) |
| Team setups | `const COMPS = [...]` |
| Map blurbs | `const MAPS = [...]` |
| Map defaults table | `const MAPDEF = [...]` |
| Map record | `const MAPLOG = [...]` |
| Acronyms, site clusters, prep questions, queue hypotheses, tips | the matching static HTML sections |
| Look / print CSS / card markup | only if the user asked for a design change |

Inside `P`, each player has: `name`, `role`, `summary`, `strip` (career figures), `srv` + `note` (survival gauge), `s` / `w` / `g` / `a` (prose bullet arrays), `atk` / `def` (top-five tables), and `tst_a` / `tst_d` (experiment blocks, or the string `"na"`).

Local tracker PDFs live in `pdfs/`. That folder is gitignored so it is never pushed. Read those files when refreshing operator tables. Do not add `pdfs/` to `.cursorignore`.

`rough-notes.md` is gitignored. Local working notes, tests, and the handle-to-name map. Do not commit it, and never copy real names from it onto the page, into commits, or into README.

Top-five attack and defence order uses the equal-weight score in `README.md` (win rate, K/D, survival, rounds — 25% each), compared inside sample-size bands (30+ / 11–29 / ≤10). Do not sort by rounds alone.

After a content update, bump `<meta name="document-version">` to `YYYY-MM-DD-HHMM` in local time.

## Reading the tracker PDFs

Tracker exports in `pdfs/` are the source of truth for operator tables. Read them properly — most errors in this project have come from working off the top of a table or a screenshot.

**Extract the whole table, every time.**

- The operator list is paginated and the column header row repeats on each page and sometimes mid-table. Do not stop at the first page, and do not treat a repeated header as a data row.
- Operator names wrap across lines in some exports (a two-word name can land with the name on one line and its figures on the next). Handle wrapped rows rather than dropping them.
- Capture every column: rounds, wins, win rate, K/D, opening kills and opening deaths, headshot percentage, survival, and 1vX wins. Opening records and survival are frequently the most informative columns and are the ones most often skipped.
- Go all the way to the bottom. Low-round operators are where new candidates live; the project's best finds have come from rows near the end of the table, not the top.

**Then sanity-check before using it.**

- Cross-check the profile summary — matches, career kills, K/D, win rate, headshot rate — against what you extracted. If they disagree, the extraction is wrong, not the tracker.
- The export does not label attackers and defenders. Classify each operator yourself before splitting into `atk` and `def`.
- Compare against the values currently in the page. A figure that has moved a long way in a short time usually means a parsing error, a different season filter, or a different playlist filter — check before writing it in.

**Picking the best operators.**

- Score with the equal-weight method in `README.md` (win rate, K/D, survival, rounds), compared **inside** sample-size bands.
- Surface candidates the page does not already list: operators with a strong line and low volume, operators whose role fills a gap in the pool, and operators whose figures have moved meaningfully since the last export.
- Report what you found and why, including the band each candidate sits in, before editing any table.

| Metric | Very poor | Poor | Fair | Good | Excellent |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **K/D — fragging role** | < 0.55 | 0.55–0.79 | 0.80–1.04 | 1.05–1.34 | ≥ 1.35 |
| **K/D — support role** | < 0.40 | 0.40–0.59 | 0.60–0.84 | 0.85–1.09 | ≥ 1.10 |
| **Win rate** | < 38% | 38–44% | 45–52% | 53–59% | ≥ 60% |
| **Survival — attack** | < 20% | 20–29% | 30–39% | 40–47% | ≥ 48% |
| **Survival — defence** | < 18% | 18–25% | 26–33% | 34–42% | ≥ 43% |
| **Headshot %** | < 20% | 20–27% | 28–35% | 36–44% | ≥ 45% |
| **Opening ratio** (entry kills ÷ entry deaths) | < 0.40 | 0.40–0.79 | 0.80–1.24 | 1.25–2.49 | ≥ 2.50 |

**Which K/D row applies.** Use the **support** row when the player's career K/D sits below 1.00, or when their role is explicitly support, utility, intel or anchor. Use the **fragging** row otherwise. Choose the row from the *player*, not the operator, and apply it consistently across their whole card.

**Not everyone is a natural fragger, and that is not a deficiency.** Some players contribute through position, utility and objective play rather than through duels. On those cards the kill column is a weak signal and the win rate is the strong one. The document's own central finding says so directly: utility that keeps working after death converts rounds without the player needing to be alive for it. Judging a support player on the fragging scale produces a card that is uniformly negative and analytically wrong.

**Win rate bands are deliberately tight.** Matchmaking pulls everyone toward 50%, so a few points either side is a large real effect. Do not treat a 45% and a 58% as similar.

**Opening ratio needs at least eight combined opening engagements to read.** Below that, report the raw split and say nothing about it.

**Always sanity-check against the player's own career line as well as the absolute band.** The same figure means different things on different cards. Say which comparison you are making.

**Do not describe a one-band gap as decisive.** Fair versus good is a nudge. Very poor versus good is a finding.

- **A support-role card reading negative across the board.** Almost always means the fragging K/D row was applied by mistake. Re-band it before writing anything.

## Voice and method

Write like the existing page: plain, specific, slightly dry. No hype, no ranking of the five, no "tier lists." **Chat the same way.** Do not save the riddles for the Discord message and the plain words for the page. If it needs a glossary to parse, it is wrong.

- Cards are deliberately unranked.
- Career figures unless labelled otherwise.
- **Prose carries no numbers.** The `s`, `w`, `g` and `a` arrays must contain no figures at all. Numbers belong in the tables directly beneath them, and only there. This rule exists because duplicated figures fall out of sync on every single update. Use a qualitative scale instead: **very poor · poor · fair · good · excellent**. Write *"excellent K/D and survival, with two aces behind them"*, never *"2.46 K/D across 27 rounds"*. Superlatives such as *"best line on the card"* are allowed but age faster than band words, so use them sparingly.
- Sample size still governs how strongly the *tables* are read: ten rounds or fewer is noise (mark `REC` with the 7th operator-row flag `1`), 11–30 is a hint, 30+ can support a conclusion. Twenty rounds spread across sessions is the bar for promoting an experiment into a top five.
- Small samples are volatile and large ones are anchored. An operator's K/D can be transformed by one good run below roughly twenty-five rounds and is effectively fixed above forty. Never promise improvement the arithmetic forbids.
- **Never compare across sample-size bands as if the numbers were equivalent.** A five-round line does not "beat" a sixty-round line, whatever the percentages say. Ranking, promotion and any "X over Y" claim happen *inside* a band, never across one. Writing "more Clash beats more solo Azami" when Clash has a handful of rounds and Azami has dozens is exactly the error to avoid: the percentages are not measuring the same thing, so the comparison is meaningless rather than merely uncertain.
- **What a small sample can support:** that an operator is worth more rounds, that an early line is encouraging, that it fits a role the pool lacks. **What it cannot support:** that it is better than an established pick, that it should displace one, or that it solves a diagnosed problem. Phrase small-sample claims as reasons to test, never as findings.
- **Do not dress a recommendation as a finding.** "Worth trying Clash for the survival problem" is honest. "Clash beats Azami on survival" is a claim the data cannot carry. If the evidence is a hunch about role fit, say so.
- Hypotheses stay hypotheses until the log actually proves them. One session is not a law.
- Operator rows are `[name, rounds, WR, K/D, SRV, note, isRec?]`. Keep the five-plus-experiment shape per side.
- **If the export has a row, write the figures.** Dashes (`—`) are only for operators with no sample on the account. A two-round 4.00 K/D is still a 4.00 K/D. Mark it `REC` because the sample is tiny; do not blank it. Blanking a real row is how kills disappear from the page.
- Operator notes: hard cap **12 words**, **78 characters with spaces**, **67 without**. That is three lines in the table. **No em dashes inside them** — use commas or full stops. No dated figures; they go stale. The same cap applies to experiment-row notes.
- Strengths / weaknesses / suggestions / avoid are HTML strings in arrays. Lead with `<b>claim</b> — explanation.` Skimming the bold labels alone should be useful.
- Weaknesses describe playstyle, ability and behaviour, not what happened on a given date. Session events belong in queue hypotheses.
- If a figure changes, update every copy of it: `P.strip`, `P.srv` / `P.note` if survival moved, `ROSTER`, and the operator row. There should be no fourth copy anywhere.

## Voice and tone

The severity of a judgement is set by the figures. The *framing* around it is a choice, and it is the framing that has gone wrong before, not the vocabulary.

**Do not euphemise.** Keep the honest band words — very poor, poor, fair, good, excellent. Softening "poor" to "below fair" undersells a real finding and makes the document less useful. If something is genuinely bad, say poor.

**Rate the evidence, do not sentence the operator.** The line is between describing what the numbers show and issuing a verdict on what should happen next. The first is analysis. The second reads as scolding, and it is usually overreach anyway, because the reader decides what to play.

### Avoid

- **Imperatives that sound like telling off** — "Stop growing the EMP pick", "Drop him", "Leave it alone".
- **Superlative rankings** — "the worst line in the squad", "the weakest operator on the card". They are rarely load-bearing, they age badly, and they read as a pile-on.
- **Finality** — "it's over", "off this list", "on the shelf", "on the bench", "writing her off", "deal-breaker". Nothing in this document is a closed case.
- **Stacking negatives in one clause** — "poor conversion, a very poor gun line, and he dies on it" lands as three insults rather than one finding.
- **Weakness stated as identity** — "he dies on it" describes a person. "Survival is poor" describes a figure.
- **Snark of any kind.** It is never worth the goodwill it costs.

### Use instead

Structures that carry the same information without the verdict:

- *enough rounds to read* — the sample is sufficient, without pronouncing sentence
- *not a priority* — deprioritised, not condemned
- *worth waiting for a larger sample* — honest about uncertainty
- *the card has stronger options* — comparative, not absolute
- *the squad has better uses for the time* — reframes as opportunity cost
- *the primary weapon is the blocker* — names the mechanism rather than the outcome

### Worked example

> ✗ Poor conversion, a very poor gun line, and he dies on it. Stop growing the EMP pick.
>
> ✓ Conversion and survival are both poor, and the squad has better uses for the time.

Same severity. Same conclusion available to the reader. No imperative, no pile-on, no verdict.

### The test

Every line in this document could be read aloud to the player it describes. Findings survive that; snark does not. Apply it equally to all five cards — the owner's own card gets no harsher treatment than anyone else's, and no gentler.

**Tone changes the framing, never the content.** This section is not licence to soften a real finding, bury a bad number, or avoid a conclusion the figures support. Say the hard thing plainly and once, then move on to what could be done about it.

## Map framework

Five site clusters. These are **axes, not boxes** — most sites belong to two.

1. **Sub-level / basement** — attacked from above only. Hatch denial and anti-Fuze cover.
2. **Top floor / roof-exposed** — rappellers, windows, hatches. Exterior angles matter more than interior ones.
3. **Open mid-floor / long sightline** — too many entrances to deny. Access denial fails here, and anything built around a close-range gun is a liability past fifteen metres.
4. **Tight interior / CQB** — fights decided inside ten metres. Rewards close-range weapons.
5. **Linear / corridor** — forced pathing, hard breach rarely needed, trap value at its highest.

**Prep-phase check, in this order, every round:**

1. Am I below everything?
2. Am I above everything?
3. How many entrances? Two or three, take access denial. Five or more, take cameras and intel.
4. How long is the longest angle? Under fifteen metres, close-range picks are live.

## Durable findings

These have survived multiple sessions and should inform recommendations.

- **Defensive win rate can exceed attacking win rate even with a worse K/D and worse survival.** Utility that keeps working after you die converts rounds without needing you alive. This is the most important structural insight in the project, and it justifies keeping trap and denial operators whose personal figures look unimpressive.
- **Roaming and flanking scale with room size.** Small rooms and tight staircases reward it. Large open interiors punish it, because the flank crosses open ground.
- **Playlist can explain map results.** Maps that look bad in casual play sometimes produce comfortable wins in ranked, where rounds are slower and fuller. Check playlist before condemning a map.
- **After a bad casual loss, switch to ranked.** Repeatedly observed. A casual loss is often the tell that concentration has slipped, and ranked's structure corrects it.
- **Three losses in a row, stop for the night.** No debate. Every long negative session in the log continued well past the point it was salvageable.
- **Do not repeat an entry.** The same rappel window twice, or breaking windows in a fixed order, gets read. What works against bots gets punished by people.
- **Smoky bacon works.** Thermite plus Capitão, confirmed in real matches: Capitão runs Impact EMPs, Thermite runs smoke. EMPs strip the wall denial, the wall goes up, smoke goes into the hole, both enter together, fire bolts deny the room behind.
- **Controller configuration matters more than expected.** Input deadzones, the sensitivity split between axes, and field of view have all produced larger swings than operator changes.

## How to behave

- **Talk like a teammate after a session, not a briefing note.** Informal British English. Humour is fine. Rigour stays. If a sentence would make someone say "wtf does that mean", rewrite it before sending.
- **Answer the actual question first.** If they asked whether something is possible, start with yes or no, then the condition. Do not open with "not a law this sample can carry", "the evidence cannot support", or any other analyst riddle. Say "yes, if you stop after three losses" or "no, not with that conversion" or "too few games to know yet."
- **Fun is a real reason to play someone.** Enjoyment produces rounds, and rounds produce the line. A lower score is not a closed door when they like the operator and the figures are moving. Do not talk them out of a pick they enjoy unless the line is actually poor and stuck. Yield the seat. Log it as a judgement call, not as the score losing.
- **Challenge the user when enthusiasm outruns evidence.** Still do this. Just do it in plain English, once, without sounding like a veto.
- **Do not override the user's tactical judgement with a number and no explanation.** They know Siege well. Where their call conflicts with the data, say the tension in one sentence and let them decide.
- **Verify game mechanics before asserting them.** Operator kits, weapon statistics and patch history change constantly. Cite sources. Where unsure, say so plainly.
- **Be kind in weaknesses.** Frame problems as habits rather than limitations, and pair each with what fixing it would achieve. Never state a weakness as a fixed trait.
- **Keep suggestions few and forward-looking.** Roll-calls of what is already in the tables are clutter. Every bullet should name something to do or something not already obvious.
- **Recommendations should argue for themselves.** Do not simply record instructions back into the document — make the case, so it can be disagreed with.
- **Weekly updates beat per-session ones.** One session is noise, a week is a sample. Discourage late-night update spirals.

## Validation

Before handing back any change to the script:

1. Extract the `<script>` body, stub `document.getElementById`, and run it under node. It must execute without error and render no `undefined`.
2. Check the prose arrays contain no digits.
3. Render the page and **look at it**. Column widths, wrapping and spacing have been wrong before and were only caught by eye.

## Do not

- Do not replace embedded fonts or dump a new full-page export over the file unless the user is explicitly swapping the whole page.
- Do not add analytics, comments that explain the secret URL, or indexable copies of the page at a guessable path.
- Do not silently change content unrelated to the request. If a change has a knock-on effect elsewhere, make it and say so.
- Do not commit or push unless asked.